<h3 id="should_you_upgrade">Should You Upgrade?</h3><br/>
If your 2021 model still feels responsive and lasts long enough for reading, browsing, and taking notes, keeping it makes sense for now, and if battery life is your only complaint, a battery service may be enough.<br/>
<br/>
However, for many 2021 &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj; owners, the 2026 model is a worthwhile upgrade. If you've had yours since launch, its battery has had five years to wear down, and 64GB can feel cramped if you keep games or downloaded video on it. The new model gives you a fresh battery and twice the storage if you're coming from the 64GB version. Apple still rates both models for up to 10 hours when new, but the 2026 model can charge to 50% in around 30 minutes with a 35W or higher adapter.<br/>
<br/>
The &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; display and lighter design are the changes you're most likely to notice every day. The screen can get dimmer for reading in bed and brighter for HDR video, and the lower weight makes the &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj; easier to hold one-handed for long stretches. Both displays run at 60Hz, with no ProMotion. The A20 Pro and extra memory matter more if you play demanding games, edit photos or video, or often find that apps reload when you switch back to them.<br/>
<br/>
Some of the smaller changes matter more depending on where you use your &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj;. IP67 water and dust resistance is useful if you take it outdoors, read by the pool, or use it in the kitchen. The landscape front camera is better positioned for calls when the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a> is propped up horizontally, and extended display support lets you use a monitor as a separate workspace.<br/>
<br/>
<h3 id="which_model_should_you_buy">Which Model Should You Buy?</h3><br/>
The discontinued <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/ipad-mini-2024-vs-2026/">2024 iPad mini</a> is a good middle ground if your 2021 model is showing its age and you're happy with its display. It gives you more base storage, &zwnj;Apple Intelligence&zwnj;, faster USB-C transfers, and &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj; Pro support, and you can reuse your existing Smart Folio. Apple lists the 128GB Wi-Fi model for &#36;509 through its Certified Refurbished store when available, with a new battery and a one-year warranty. Remaining new stock from third-party retailers is also worth considering at a low enough price.<br/>
<br/>
If prices are close at the same storage capacity, buy the 2026 model. At &#36;699, it makes the most sense if you specifically want the &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; display, lighter body, or water resistance. If you use a Smart Folio, factor in &#36;79 for the redesigned version. The USB-C &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj; works with either newer model, while the second-generation &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj; needs replacing. Both newer cellular models use eSIM, so check with your carrier if you currently use a physical SIM.<br/>
<br/>
A used 2021 &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj; is still a reasonable buy at around &#36;300 or lower if you want a second device for reading, notes, or streaming and 64GB is enough. Battery condition matters more than cosmetic wear; if the price of a 256GB unit or the cost of a battery service brings it close to a refurbished 2024 model, the newer device is still the better buy.<br/>
<br/>
